> ## Recommended v1 design: three explicit execution plans
> 
> Treat this as selecting among *bounded policy variants*, not dynamically deciding inside a responder:
> 
> 1. **`direct_with_plan`**  
>    Call the LLM immediately. Require it to produce the user-facing answer plus a small structured “research plan” internally (e.g. whether a search would materially improve/correct it). No search latency.
> 
> 2. **`rag_web_search`**  
>    Run one or more predefined searches first, inject selected source excerpts, then call the LLM. Use for freshness-sensitive, factual, high-stakes, or source-requesting questions.
> 
> 3. **`parallel_direct_and_search`**  
>    Start the direct LLM call and web searches concurrently. Return the direct answer if search is not ready/material; otherwise synthesize a grounded answer. This needs an async/threaded internal executor even if `Planner` remains synchronous externally.
> 
> For a true v1 synchronous implementation, initially support only (1) and (2); represent (3) as a future `WorkflowResponder`.
> 
> ## Immediate code repairs
> 
> Before adding policy logic:
> 
> - Fix `ToolUseResponder.chat_response`: its signature lacks `record`, but passes an undefined `record` to `super()`.
> - Implement `ToolUseResponder.stream_response`; otherwise tool-bound plans inherit direct streaming and never invoke tools.
> - Do not mutate `self.message_builder` per request: it is unsafe if responders are reused concurrently. Pass effective messages explicitly or construct a per-request responder.
> - Persist `ToolResult.artifacts` and source excerpts before model invocation.
> - Generate web-search arguments automatically: default `{"query": latest_user_message}` unless a query-rewriter is later introduced.
> - Reconcile imports in `openai_web_search.py`: it must use the canonical `planner.types` `Artifact`, `ToolInvocation`, `ToolResult`, and `ToolConfig`.
> 
> ## Fast local feature extraction
> 
> Add immutable `query_features` to `PlannerContext` and store it in `ResponseRecord`.
> 
> A local `QueryAnalyzer` should extract sparse features such as:
> 
> - lexical cues: `latest`, year/date, `news`, `current`, `price`, `law`, `citation`, `source`;
> - interrogative/task cues: comparison, recommendation, research, troubleshooting;
> - temporal entities, URLs, named organizations/products;
> - question length and uncertainty;
> - risk class / explicit grounding requirement.
> 
> Avoid simplistic triggers such as “what is” or “how to”; they would search far too often.
> 
> ## Bayesian learned policy
> 
> Maintain evidence by `(plan_id, feature)` with Beta priors:
> 
> \[
> \theta_{p,f} \sim \mathrm{Beta}(\alpha_{p,f}, \beta_{p,f})
> \]
> 
> Use a bounded feature vocabulary (word unigrams/bigrams plus engineered features). For each plan, estimate expected satisfaction/quality using a Naive-Bayes-like additive log-odds model, with shrinkage toward plan-wide evidence.
> 
> Selection becomes:
> 
> \[
> EU(p|x)=
> w_s E[\mathrm{satisfaction}]
> +w_q E[\mathrm{quality}]
> -w_c E[\mathrm{cost}]
> -w_l E[\mathrm{latency}]
> -w_r E[\mathrm{risk}]
> +w_e \mathrm{exploration}
> \]
> 
> Use Thompson sampling or upper-confidence bounds only where `RiskPolicy.allow_exploration` permits it.
> 
> On `vote_up`/`vote_down`, update the selected plan’s global and active-feature Beta counts. Keep thumbs separate from tool failures, grounding evaluation, and factuality checks.
> 
> ## Parallel-plan workflow
> 
> Implement `parallel_direct_and_search` in `workflow`:
> 
> - create independent `model_call` and `web_search` operations;
> - stream direct output only after a short “search grace period” or mark it provisional;
> - if visible output began, never silently replace it;
> - deliver a later cited correction/update only when search produces materially different information.
> 
> This preserves the synchronous API while making the concurrency semantics explicit and auditable.
