>   "queries": [],
>   "reason": "Stable astronomical timescale; web search unlikely to materially change answer.",
>   "urgency": "none"
> }
> ```
> 
> That is a successful direct-plan decision, not a search failure.
**user**:
> When should I use Planner.explain()? And how does that compare to current_plan_text()?
**assistant**:
> Use them at different times and for different scopes:
> 
> - **`current_plan_text()`**: during local debugging/UI display, for the **most recently selected plan on that `Planner` instance**. It is concise and human-readable.
> - **`explain(response_id)`**: after a response exists (and ideally after a stream finishes), for durable inspection of **any specific response**. It reads the persisted `ResponseRecord`, so it remains correct across requests, instances, and restarts when using durable persistence.
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```python
> response = planner.chat_response(messages)
> 
> print(planner.current_plan_text())   # quick “what is happening now?”
> print(planner.explain(response.id))  # durable structured attribution
> ```
> 
> For streaming:
> 
> ```python
> stream = planner.stream_response(messages)
> 
> print(planner.current_plan_text())  # valid immediately: selected plan/response ID
> text = "".join(stream)              # finish stream so metrics are finalized
> 
> details = planner.explain(stream.id)
> print(details)
> ```
> 
> Comparison:
> 
> | Method | Input | Scope | Format | Best use |
> |---|---|---|---|---|
> | `current_plan_text()` | none | Latest plan in this planner object | text graph | terminal/debug panel |
> | `current_plan()` | none | Latest plan in this planner object | mapping | UI/API debugging |
> | `explain(response_id)` | response ID | Specific persisted response | mapping | logging, feedback, auditing |
> 
> For the three-way logic, add these fields to `explain()`:
> 
> ```python
> "execution_mode": plan.metadata.get("execution_mode", "direct"),
> "decision_features": list(record.decision_features),
> "visible_output_started": record.visible_output_started,
> ```
> 
> Then use:
> 
> ```python
> details = planner.explain(response.id)
> print(details["execution_mode"])
> print(details["decision_features"])
> ```
> 
> That tells you why the planner had the relevant inputs to consider web search. Note that it does not yet expose **per-plan scores** or the rejected alternatives; add a separate `DecisionRecord` if you want a complete “why this plan rather than RAG?” audit trail.
